<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip I of Taranto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1278–1331</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip I of Taranto was an Angevin prince, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles II of Naples</a>, who received the Principality of Taranto in 1294. He also held the Angevin claims to the Kingdom of Albania and the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> and, through his marriage to Catherine of Valois, the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. His efforts to expand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> achieved little.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-i-of-taranto.jpg" alt="Philip I of Taranto" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Villani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Filippo-dangio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
